these families left venezuela hoping for  a new life  in chile back home life had become  unbearable  they say there's no food no jobs and no  future  you can't survive in venezuela you  really can't how are we supposed to make  it with about three dollars a month  now there are fears of a potential  humanitarian crisis  entire communities are walking towards  the chilean border  climbing mountains and rocky grounds to  avoid border checkpoints  at least two people died attempting the  journey one from colombia  the other from venezuela  about 30 people who were in the group  tried to help one of the migrants with  mouth-to-mouth resuscitation  but it's possible the patient had  covered nineteen so now  there's a risk the virus has spread more  than four million people have fled  venezuela in recent years  most went to colombia and peru but now  more families are choosing to go to  prosperous chile  but not everyone is happy to see them  families from our local aimaras  indigenous group have had intruders in  their properties  they walk in looking for food and  clothes they go through peru and they  arrive to chile in deplorable conditions  chilean soldiers have been deployed  along bolivia's border to prevent  illegal crossings  but stopping them has been a challenge  most refugees and migrants say their  desire  for a new start is stronger than the  fear of the dangerous journey  katya lopez odeon al jazeera
 

